# instructions on updating a pull request

this command is for **updating an existing standard pull request** targeting
the `release` branch after last-minute code changes, rule changes, or scope
adjustments.

before making any update decision, always read and apply the rules in
[versioning.md](../guides/versioning.md). use that guide to decide whether the
new commits still justify the pr as-is or whether the pr should be reframed,
split, or deferred.

use this command when:

- new commits were added after the PR draft was created
- business rules changed, such as access rules, permissions, billing logic, or other behavior-sensitive code
- the PR description, title, or checklist needs to reflect the latest implementation context

## to-do list

1. Read [versioning.md](../guides/versioning.md) and compare the current
   branch state against the existing pr intent.
2. Inspect the new commits since the original PR context was created.
3. Determine whether the PR still represents one coherent change.
4. If the scope changed, update the pr title, description, and checklist to
   reflect the new reality.
5. If the code changes invalidate the previous pr direction, tell the user
   before proceeding.
6. [human-in-the-loop] Show the updated PR context to the user and ask for approval.
7. Only after approval, update the pull request using the github mcp.

## what to re-evaluate

- whether the pr still belongs in `release`
- whether the new commits change the feature, bugfix, or rule interpretation
- whether access, permissions, or business rules changed in a way that should
  be explained clearly
- whether the pr should mention the latest commit set instead of the original
  one

## important

- do not silently overwrite pr context without checking the latest commits.
- prefer updating the existing pr description over creating a new pr when the
  change is still part of the same work.
- if the scope has drifted too far, stop and ask the user whether to keep
  updating the existing pr or split the work.
